=== feature8_1_push_scalar ===
{
  "description": "Appending an item to the array grows its length by one and renders a corresponding input bound to the new indexed field. Editing the value of an existing item's input updates only that item and leaves later appended items empty. After appending one item, editing it, then appending a second, the array length is 2 and the two inputs hold the edited value and an empty string respectively. The input names follow the parent-name-with-bracketed-index convention.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "array_state",
        "name": "foo",
        "subscription": {},
        "initialValues": {"foo": []},
        "itemFields": [""],
        "actions": [
          {"type": "push", "value": null},
          {"type": "change", "name": "foo[0]", "value": "dog"},
          {"type": "push", "value": null}
        ],
        "report": ["length", "inputs"]
      },
      "expected_output": "length=2\ninputs=2\nfoo[0]=dog\nfoo[1]=\n"
    }
  ]
}

Edge semantics, stated once: for feature4, length must be present on the bundle regardless of how narrow the subscription is — a single unrelated flag like 'dirty' still exposes the correct length, and a pristine untouched array reports dirty=false. For feature8_1, the 'change' action targets an indexed field name (foo[0]) and mutates only that item; the second push appends an empty item that the earlier edit did not touch. Field name format is strictly parentName + '[' + index + ']'. Note the report line ordering in expected_output follows the 'report' array order — length and inputs counts first, then per-input name=value lines. Remaining files if you need them: feature8_2_push_object, feature9_resubscribe_on_name_change. Ask by name.

