=== feature8_2_push_object ===
{
  "description": "Items may be objects whose sub-properties are bound to independent nested inputs. Appending an empty object renders one input per declared sub-field, each named with the parent item path followed by a dotted sub-property name. Editing one sub-field updates only that sub-field and leaves the sibling sub-field empty. After appending one object item and editing its first sub-field, the array length is 1 and the two nested inputs hold the edited value and an empty string.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "array_state",
        "name": "foo",
        "subscription": {},
        "initialValues": {"foo": []},
        "itemFields": ["firstName", "lastName"],
        "actions": [
          {"type": "push", "value": {}},
          {"type": "change", "name": "foo[0].firstName", "value": "Erik"}
        ],
        "report": ["length", "inputs"]
      },
      "expected_output": "length=1\ninputs=2\nfoo[0].firstName=Erik\nfoo[0].lastName=\n"
    }
  ]
}

=== feature9_resubscribe_on_name_change ===
{
  "description": "The array field re-subscribes to a different array when its target name changes, so its reported value follows the named array. With one form holding two named arrays, the field bound to the first name reports that array's value; after the field's name is switched to the second array, it reports the second array's value.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "array_state",
        "name": "dogs",
        "formSubscription": {},
        "initialValues": {"dogs": ["Odie"], "cats": ["Garfield"]},
        "report": ["value"]
      },
      "expected_output": "value=[\"Odie\"]\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "array_state",
        "name": "dogs",
        "formSubscription": {},
        "initialValues": {"dogs": ["Odie"], "cats": ["Garfield"]},
        "actions": [
          {"type": "set_name", "name": "cats"}
        ],
        "report": ["value"]
      },
      "expected_output": "value=[\"Garfield\"]\n"
    }
  ]
}

Edge semantics, stated once: for feature8_2, object items use dot notation — 'parentName[index].subFieldName' (e.g. foo[0].firstName). Each sub-field is an independent input; editing one leaves siblings untouched. The 'itemFields' array drives which sub-fields get inputs per pushed object. For feature9, the binding must detect the name difference itself (the set_name action) and re-register with the form engine for the new field — value and state reflect the newly named array after the switch; no manual re-wiring. Note feature9 uses 'formSubscription' rather than 'subscription' in its input shape — handle both in your adapter.

