=== feature6_map ===
{
  "description": "The field bundle exposes a mapping helper that invokes a callback once per array item, in order, passing the indexed field name (parent name with a bracketed index suffix) and the zero-based position, and collects the callback's return values into a new array preserving order. Each emitted line records one visited name and its index; the final line reports the JSON-encoded array of results (here the upper-cased indexed names).",
  "cases": [
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "map",
        "name": "foo",
        "initialValues": {"foo": ["a", "b", "c"]}
      },
      "expected_output": "foo[0]=0\nfoo[1]=1\nfoo[2]=2\nresult=[\"FOO[0]\",\"FOO[1]\",\"FOO[2]\"]\n"
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "op": "map",
        "name": "foo",
        "initialValues": {"foo": ["a", "b"]}
      },
      "expected_output": "foo[0]=0\nfoo[1]=1\nresult=[\"FOO[0]\",\"FOO[1]\"]\n"
    }
  ]
}

Edge semantics, stated once: both helpers invoke the callback once per item in order, passing two arguments — the indexed field name (strictly parentName + '[' + index + ']', zero-based) and the numeric zero-based index. for_each returns nothing (undefined) and that absence is reported explicitly as return=undefined. map collects each callback return value into a NEW ordered array — the original array is not mutated — and reports it JSON-encoded. The upper-casing you see in the map results is the callback the harness applies; your core just needs the helper contract correct.

